/*
:name: concat_bundle_lanes
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: concat_bundle_lanes.top
:pyrope_top: top
*/

// RTL coverage for the `concat(a, b, c)` builtin (docs/pyrope/10-internals.md,
// "Bit selection and concatenation"). The comptime tier is covered by
// ../pyrope/concat_two_lanes.prp and the rejection rules by ../errors/concat_*;
// this pair is the one that proves the LOWERED datapath against an independent
// Verilog statement of the same layout, over scalar lanes AND over the fields
// of a BUNDLE input port. concat_tuple_lanes independently covers direct dotted
// lanes and proves that their explicit order controls the packed layout.
//
// Each output pins one claim:
//
//   zu   MSB-FIRST, per-lane DECLARED windows. `concat(h,l,c)` is
//        `{p.hi, p.lo, c}` — 4+3+2 = 9 bits, argument 0 in the high bits, from
//        a bundle port's two fields plus a scalar port.
//
//   zs   the SAME concat into a SIGNED destination of the same width. `s9` and
//        `u9` are both legal (only the 9-bit field width is checked, never the
//        signedness) and carry the same bits, so `zs` must equal `zu` and the
//        two ports differ only in how a reader reads them.
//
//   wu   widening the unsigned destination: zero-extend, 9 -> 12.
//   ws   widening the SIGNED destination — also a ZERO-extend, and that is the
//        point of having both. A concat result is non-negative BY CONSTRUCTION
//        (every lane masks into its own window, so no lane's sign can escape),
//        so `s9` is a container, not a reinterpretation: `ws` is `wu`, not the
//        sign-extension of bit 8. Pyrope is value-based here — `const s:S9 = a`
//        for an `a:U9` keeps `a`'s value, it does not re-read its top bit as a
//        sign — and a design that read `zs` back as negative would refute.
//
//   nest `concat(concat(h,l), c)` == `concat(h,l,c)`. Nesting is legal because
//        the inner result's width is its lane sum by construction (7 bits), so
//        the inner temp needs no user-written declaration to be a lane.
//
//   kz   the width is the lane's DECLARED type, never its VALUE: `k:U4` holds
//        1, and the lane is still FOUR bits (`4'b0001`), not the one bit the
//        value needs. Sized by value it would be `{1'b1, c}` — a 3-bit answer
//        in a 6-bit port, i.e. every bit above `c` shifted.
//
//   sx   the signedness CONTRAST, and the reason the bundle carries a signed
//        field. `l:S3` used as a VALUE sign-extends (sx is `l` in 9 bits); the
//        same `l` used as a concat LANE contributes exactly its own 3-bit
//        two's-complement window and never reaches past it (bits [4:2] of zu).
//        An emitter that sign-extended a signed lane would keep sx right and
//        silently corrupt zu.
//
// `zu`/`zs`/`wu`/`ws` share one concat and `nest` feeds a concat into another
// concat's lane, so BOTH cgen paths are exercised: the inlined `{...}` (kz, sx)
// and the materialized net `Cgen_verilog::create_locals` mints when a Concat has
// several readers or feeds a lane (`reg [9:0] concat_16;`). This pair is LEC'd
// by re-reading that emitted file, so it also gates the round trip through it.
//
// The bundle fields are rebound to declared locals (`h`, `l`) so this fixture
// also covers ordinary typed local lanes. Direct `concat(p.hi, p.lo, c)` is
// legal and covered by concat_tuple_lanes; the field-read temps recover their
// declared widths through tuple-get provenance.
//
// `prp-v2prp2v-concat_bundle_lanes` FAILS today and is deliberately UNTAGGED
// (no `fixme`, so it stays visible): the prp_writer emits `concat` lanes as raw
// EXPRESSIONS and literals —
//   const t4 = concat(0, packed_bits__w1)
// — which is exactly what the lane rule rejects, so the Verilog -> Pyrope ->
// Verilog round trip dies on the re-read, untyped destination and all. The fix
// is in upass/prp_writer: bind each lane to a typed name and type the
// destination. The two forward directions (`prp-equiv-`, `prp-statematch-`) are
// green.
pub comb top(p:(hi:U4, lo:S3), c:U2) -> (zu:U9, zs:S9, wu:U12, ws:S12, nest:U9, kz:U6, sx:S9) {
  const h:U4 = p.hi
  const l:S3 = p.lo
  const k:U4 = 1     // value needs 1 bit; the LANE is the declared 4

  const packed_u:U9 = (c, l, h)#[..]   // 4+3+2 = 9
  const packed_s:S9 = (c, l, h)#[..]   // same entries, signed destination

  zu   = packed_u
  zs   = packed_s
  wu   = packed_u                 // 9 -> 12: zero-extend
  ws   = packed_s                 // 9 -> 12: ALSO zero-extend
  nest = (c, (l, h)#[..])#[..]    // (4+3) + 2 = 9, same layout as zu
  kz   = (c, k)#[..]              // 4+2 = 6
  sx   = l                        // the same signed field as a VALUE: sign-extended
}
